From 3d9da2d8f904b12b75beeedd596604fd5cf018fd Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 23:28:37 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [Blu-ray] Tell the BD-J application what the reader passed
 when the picture gets there

The Kodi side of libbluray-9072/9073 (SamuriHL's libbluray patch 13 and the
changes to it). Kodi reads up to the depth of its buffers ahead of the picture,
and libbluray told the BD-J application of play marks, playitem and chapter
changes and the end of a playlist as the reader passed them: the application
lived that far in the future (14 s on M3GAN 2.0's menu loop, per SamuriHL).

Navigation playback enables libbluray's presentation timing before bd_play()
(libbluray applies it to BD-J titles alone). After each read the newest number
libbluray holds goes to the player (BD_EVENT_BDJ_PRESENTATION_STAMP), which
stamps it with the last packet it handed on - where the reading is, a little
before it by what the demuxer buffers, so released early rather than late - and
releases it when its clock gets there. Due at once: no time stamped, or a stamp
further ahead than any buffer holds (another timeline). A flush (seek, jump,
playlist stop) and a new input stream release everything, as without it.

At the end of a BD-J playlist Read() waits inside its loop for the
application, which now hears of the end only when the picture gets there, and
this thread is the one that tells libbluray so: the wait asks the player to
release what its clock reached and sleeps 5 ms instead of spinning. Paused or
filling its buffers, the player's picture does not move, so a waiting read gets
everything at once; and past 20 s a read hands everything over and says so in
kodi.log, so a stalled picture cannot keep the player thread there.

Not taken from SamuriHL: polling libbluray's events while the queues are full
(his PollEvents()); here a playlist stop flushes the queues and the player
reads again as soon as a frame leaves them, so the event is seen within a
frame (read in this tree's code; not measured with a disc).

Design from SamuriHL's coreelec-xbmc 54b2c4bec6ff, commit 6e83a9fb62b2 (read as
data): the stamp after each read, the release on the clock, the wait inside
the read, the flush rule. His release runs on a disc timeline queue this tree
does not have; here it is a deque of its own in the player.

Co-developed-by: SamuriHL <samurihl@msn.com>
